"Backdoor or Manipulation? Graph Mixture of Experts Can Defend Against Various Graph Adversarial Attacks (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.15333v1)\nciteturn9521view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.15333v1\",\"lineno\":245}); Total lines: 546\nL214: When training the robustness-aware router, the process of calculating expert disagreement is $O(|\\mathcal{V}|N)$, which is linear to the number of experts, and the process of constructing the smooth label is $O(|\\mathcal{V}|N)$. In the test phase, the cost is only $O(Lh|\\mathcal{V}|d+\\mathcal{|V|}N)$ for the router and $O(Lh|\\mathcal{V}|dK)$ for the activated experts. Our time complexity analysis proves that RGMoE has great potential in conducting scalable defense on large-scale graphs.\nL215: ## 4. Experiments\nL216: \nL217: In this section, we evaluate our method on various datasets to answer the following questions:\nL218: \nL219:   * •\nL220: \nL221: RQ1: How effective is our method in defending against various GNN adversarial attacks?\nL222: \nL223:   * •\nL224: \nL225: RQ2: Does scaling the MoE capacity lead to consistent improvements in robustness and accuracy?\nL226: \nL227:   * •\nL228: \nL229: RQ3: How do the MI-based diversity loss and robustness-aware router contribute the robustness of RGMoE?\nL230: Table 1. Results under backdoor attacks. We report the attack success rate (ASR) and accuracy on clean samples.\nL231: Attacks  | Defense  | Cora  | Pubmed  | Flickr  | OGB-arxiv\nL232: ASR (%) $\\downarrow$  | ACC(%) $\\uparrow$  | ASR (%) $\\downarrow$  | ACC (%) $\\uparrow$  | ASR (%) $\\downarrow$  | ACC (%) $\\uparrow$  | ASR (%) $\\downarrow$  | ACC (%) $\\uparrow$\nL233: GTA  | GCN  | $100.00\\pm 0.00$  | $89.14\\pm 0.46$  | $99.76\\pm 0.03$  | $84.29\\pm 0.33$  | $100.00\\pm 0.00$  | $38.21\\pm 0.35$  | $95.80\\pm 0.33$  | $57.45\\pm 0.48$\nL234: GNNGuard  | $98.81\\pm 0.37$  | $79.26\\pm 0.60$  | $59.97\\pm 2.32$  | $8